As issue #64 is stale I plan to at least replace request with fetch to remove the warning that NPM shows when installing the library.

Because the existent build process is old it was causing some issues, so I replaced Rollup with esbuild and updated some dev dependencies to reduce the number of reported vulnerabilities on them to zero.

But, because of that, now the distributed code is not ES5 but ES6 and it require more modern platforms, both Node and browser. Anyway, the important thing is making the library work, adding ES5 support, if someone wishes it, will come later.

**Checklist:**

- [x] Fix backpressure in upload function which broke when fixing browser/Deno version;
    - Pausing a `PassThrough` stream, unlike npm's through, do not stop data from passing thought via a pipe;
    - Solution: implement a PassThrough stream that pauses using Transform;
- [x] Fix other issues that can arise after fixing the above;
    - [x] Uploading never calls `end` or `complete` functions
      - Solution: do **not** override Stream methods
    - [x] "Promise resolution is still pending but the event loop has already resolved." in Deno
    - [x] "spawn deno ENOENT" in CI
    - [x] "spawn npx ENOENT" in CI
    - [x] "Error: Dynamic require of "abort-controller" is not supported" in Node V14
- [x] Add test case for #83;
- [x] Add types;
- [x] Write new documentation (as the wiki do not allow versioning easily, [docusaurus](https://docusaurus.io/docs) allow);
- [x] Wait for https://github.com/standard/eslint-config-standard/issues/208 then fix issues found in files that could not be linted;
- [x] Wait for https://github.com/node-fetch/node-fetch/issues/1446 then fix eventual issues;
- [x] Add promise support
- [x] Release preview version and wait for feedback (remember to [add tag](https://stackoverflow.com/questions/39206082/npm-version-to-add-alpha-postfix));
- [ ] Release 1.0;

Hi,

I've noticed something rather odd: when using `node-fetch` and requests that have a request body either causes the request to be sent twice or being logged/intercepted twice. Note that this only happens in node versions >= 17.3.0. I'm not sure whether this is an issue with the interceptors or `node-fetch` itself. Here's the snippet I used to isolate the issue:

```js
import fetch from 'node-fetch';
import { createInterceptor } from '@mswjs/interceptors';
import nodeInterceptors from '@mswjs/interceptors/lib/presets/node.js';

const interceptor = createInterceptor({
  modules: nodeInterceptors.default,
  resolver(request, ref) {
    return {
      status: 200,
      body: JSON.stringify({
        success: true,
      }),
    };
  },
});

interceptor.apply();

interceptor.on('request', (request) => {
  console.log('[%s] %s', request.method, request.url.toString());
});

// node-fetch example taken from https://github.com/node-fetch/node-fetch#post-with-json
const body = { a: 1 };

fetch('https://httpbin.org/post', {
  method: 'POST',
  body: JSON.stringify(body),
  headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
});
```

which logs (using `--trace-warnings`):

```
[POST] https://httpbin.org/post
[POST] https://httpbin.org/post
(node:91294) MaxListenersExceededWarning: Possible EventEmitter memory leak detected. 11 end listeners added to [IncomingMessage]. Use emitter.setMaxListeners() to increase limit
    at _addListener (node:events:465:17)
    at IncomingMessage.addListener (node:events:487:10)
    at IncomingMessage.Readable.on (node:internal/streams/readable:899:35)
    at IncomingMessage.once (node:events:531:8)
    at pipe (node:internal/streams/pipeline:321:9)
    at pipelineImpl (node:internal/streams/pipeline:293:9)
    at pipeline (node:internal/streams/pipeline:146:10)
    at NodeClientRequest.<anonymous> (<project_dir>/node_modules/node-fetch/src/index.js:248:15)
    at NodeClientRequest.emit (node:events:402:35)
    at NodeClientRequest.emit (<project_dir>/node_modules/@mswjs/interceptors/lib/interceptors/ClientRequest/NodeClientRequest.js:288:46)
```
